  are wrong and I'll correct them; they're placeholders for real values, not guesses I want to bake in silently.

  [1mError handling[0m

  Two failures, both loud rather than silent:

  - [38;5;153mFORCED_ENVIRONMENT[39m set to a name not in [38;5;153mENVIRONMENTS[39m throws at load, before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m runs. A typo'd environment name
    quietly falling through to production is precisely the bug this module exists to prevent.
  - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m throws a named error if [38;5;153mwindow.APP_CONFIG[39m is missing, so a wrong script order fails with a clear message
    instead of POSTing to [38;5;153mundefined/login[39m.

  [1mChanges to [38;5;153mapp.js[0m

  The [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m constant goes away. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m composes [38;5;153m`${window.APP_CONFIG.apiBaseUrl}${LOGIN_PATH}`[39m with [38;5;153mLOGIN_PATH =
[39m  [38;5;153m"/login"[39m at the top of the file, and logs it. The function stays a stub — I'm not adding a real [38;5;153mfetch[39m; that's a
  separate request. But the stub will reference the resolved URL rather than a comment, so the config is genuinely wired
  in and observable, not decorative.

  [1mVerification[0m

  No automated tests, per your call. Manual: open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m from disk → [38;5;153mAPP_CONFIG.environment[39m is [38;5;153mdevelopment[39m;
  submitting logs [38;5;153mhttp://localhost:3000/login[39m. Then temporarily set [38;5;153mFORCED_ENVIRONMENT = "production"[39m to confirm the
  override, and a bogus value to confirm it throws.

  [1mFiles:[0m new [38;5;153msettings.js[39m; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m (one script tag plus ordering comment); [38;5;153mapp.js[39m (drop constant, read config).

  [1mOut of scope:[0m real [38;5;153mfetch[39m wiring, sharing config with [38;5;153msrc/[39m, secrets handling, tooling.

  One risk worth naming: the environment map is the only real artifact here, and [38;5;153mdevelopment[39m/[38;5;153mstaging[39m are values I made
  up. If those are wrong the module is tidier than what you had but no more useful, so it's the thing to check.

